# 2026-10-19 archive normalization: the byte-frozen subtrees keep their original
# line endings at their new location. Without these two rules a `git add` would
# rewrite CRLF to LF and the recorded evidence hashes would stop reproducing.
docs/history/archive-2026-10-19/handoffs/deepseek-r3-20260906/** -text whitespace=cr-at-eol
docs/history/archive-2026-10-19/handoffs/deepseek-r3-20260906-v2/** -text whitespace=cr-at-eol
docs/history/archive-2026-10-19/taskpacks/03_DESIGN-LAB_* -text whitespace=cr-at-eol

# Current-execution evidence is byte-frozen the same way: a receipt stores
# artifact_sha256 and the gate re-hashes the file on every read, so EOL normalisation would
# make a clean clone disagree with the ledger. CI proved this on 2026-10-12: after option A
# tracked the bytes, the failures changed from "No such file or directory" to "artifact hash
# differs", because `* text=auto eol=lf` had rewritten CRLF in the working tree to LF in the
# blob while the receipt still hashed the working-tree bytes. Evidence is measured, not
# edited -- it must reach Git exactly as the gate wrote it.
design-lab/evidence/** binary

# The five authority surfaces the DeepSeek chain pins are byte-frozen for the same reason
# design-lab/evidence/** is: the chain stores a digest and a clean clone re-checks it, so
# `* text=auto eol=lf` normalisation makes the recorded digest unreproducible off this
# machine. Observed 2026-10-13: the chain recorded the working-tree digest 620bf56d while
# the committed blob was ae368aa0 -- LF in the repository, CRLF in the checkout. The gate
# that catches this is AUTHORITY_CHAIN=DRIFT, which can never pass on a clone.
AUTHORITY.md binary
AGENTS.md binary
.project/governance/authority-index.json binary
docs/current/HISTORY-FREEZE-RULES.md binary
docs/taskpacks/DESIGN-LAB-UI-FIRST-INTEGRATED-TASKPACK-2026-10-09.md binary
docs/taskpacks/DESIGN-LAB-FINAL-AUTHORITY-CONVERGENCE-TASKPACK-2026-09-18.md binary

